## Texture generators > Noises (11 October 2026)

### Perlin Noise (11 October 2026)
- Category: Texture generators > Noises (11 October 2026)
- Height relevance: high (11 October 2026)
- Source: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/texture-generators/noises/perlin-noise (11 October 2026)
- Purpose: Smooth, widely used gradient noise whose grayscale values vary continuously across the image, with a tileable grid controlled by Scale. (11 October 2026)
- Key parameters: (11 October 2026)
  - `Scale` (Integer): Number of grid subdivisions per side, so higher values give smaller and denser features. (11 October 2026)
  - `Disorder` (Float): Moves the internal ingredients of the noise around, handy for variation or animation. (11 October 2026)
  - `Disorder speed` (Float): Distance travelled by the Disorder displacement per unit change, i.e. how fast it animates. (11 October 2026)
  - `Tile offset` (Float2): Slides the visible window over the infinite noise plane to pick another crop of the same pattern. (11 October 2026)
  - `Non-square expansion` (Boolean): Keeps cells square on non-square canvases by extending the generation to the image bounds. (11 October 2026)
- Typical use in a height graph: Soft base undulation for terrain, ground, plaster or clay heights; often warped or blended at low opacity to break flat areas before finer detail is added. (11 October 2026)
- Copernicus 22 equivalent: `fractalnoise` with **Noise Type**=Perlin and **Fractal Type**=None for one octave; **Element Size** replaces Scale (size of one grid cell in image coordinates), **Offset** replaces Tile offset, **Tile Size** makes it repeat, and the Post Processing group (**Bias**, **Gain**, **Contrast**, **Clamp Minimum**, **Clamp Maximum**) shapes the histogram. Seeds are obtained by changing **Offset** or enabling **3D Noise** and moving the third coordinate (this plays the role of Disorder, UNVERIFIED). (11 October 2026)
- Equivalence status: SideFX-confirmed at node level (the SideFX Copernicus for Substance 3D Designer users page lists this correspondence), parameter setup read from the local Houdini 22.0.368 node help (11 October 2026)

### Gaussian Noise (11 October 2026)
- Category: Texture generators > Noises (11 October 2026)
- Height relevance: high (11 October 2026)
- Source: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/texture-generators/noises/gaussian-noise (11 October 2026)
- Purpose: Smooth blobby noise built from overlapping gradients whose black to white transitions follow a bell curve, giving soft rounded lumps. (11 October 2026)
- Key parameters: (11 October 2026)
  - `Scale` (Integer): Grid subdivision of the noise tiles; larger means more and smaller lumps. (11 October 2026)
  - `Disorder` (Float): Displaces the internal pieces of the noise for variation or animation. (11 October 2026)
  - `Disorder anisotropy` (Float): Narrows the directions in which Disorder pushes the pieces (0 means any direction). (11 October 2026)
  - `Disorder anisotropy angle` (Float): Direction of the Disorder displacement once anisotropy is non-zero. (11 October 2026)
  - `Tile offset` (Float2): Chooses which crop of the infinite plane is rendered. (11 October 2026)
- Other parameters (names only): `Disorder speed`, `Non-square expansion` (11 October 2026)
- Typical use in a height graph: Go-to soft lumpy height for dunes, snow drifts, mud, cloth bulges and the large-scale pass of rock or ground before sharper noises are layered on top. (11 October 2026)
- Copernicus 22 equivalent: `fractalnoise` with **Noise Type**=Sparse Convolution (smooth value-style noise) and **Max Octaves** of 1 to 3 gives the same soft lumps; a cheaper alternative is `randommono` blurred with `blur` then stretched with `equalize`. Use **Element Size** for Scale and **Tile Size** for seamless tiling. (11 October 2026)
- Equivalence status: UNVERIFIED (mapping and parameter values inferred by me; COP node names checked against the Houdini 22.0.368 node list, parameter labels read in the local node help) (11 October 2026)

