## Texture filters > Blurs (11 October 2026)

### Slope Blur (11 October 2026)
- Category: Texture filters > Blurs (11 October 2026)
- Height relevance: high (11 October 2026)
- Source: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/blurs/slope-blur (11 October 2026)
- Purpose: High-quality blur whose direction is driven by a grayscale slope map, as if the blur followed the slopes of that map treated as a height map; internally based on Directional Warp. One of the most powerful blurs of Designer, able to chip and weather edges or smear dirt and rust. (11 October 2026)
- Inputs: Slope (Grayscale Input) (11 October 2026)
- Key parameters: (11 October 2026)
  - `Samples` (0 to 32): Number of samples (0 to 32), trading speed for quality. (11 October 2026)
  - `Intensity` (0.0 to 16.0): Blur amount (0 to 16). (11 October 2026)
  - `Mode` (Blur, Min, Max): Blending of successive passes: Blur averages, Min eats away existing areas, Max smears white areas outwards. (11 October 2026)
- Typical use in a height graph: Eroded and chipped edges (Min mode on a binary mask driven by a blurred height), streaks running down slopes, flowing dirt and rust, sedimentary smearing of cracks in a height. (11 October 2026)
- Copernicus 22 equivalent: `distort` with the Distort by Slope recipe (SideFX-confirmed): `slopedir` turns the slope height into a direction layer wired into the `dir` input input of `distort`, which pushes pixels from high toward low values. **Streak**=Blur, Min or Max matches the SD Mode (Min is described as eroding a shape, Max as adding edge roughness), **Scale** replaces Intensity, **Pixels per Step** and **Maximum Steps** replace Samples. Chain several passes for strong effects. (11 October 2026)
- Equivalence status: SideFX-confirmed at node level (the SideFX Copernicus for Substance 3D Designer users page lists this correspondence), parameter setup read from the local Houdini 22.0.368 node help (11 October 2026)

